Where the current script stands up, and where it doesn't

The good news is that the two formats most likely to be huge are already streamed. Excel uses read_only=True with row iteration, 
  so a million-row sheet never fully lands in RAM. Plain text is read line by line. Those scale to arbitrary size. PDF is extracted page
  by page, so memory is bounded by the largest single page, not the whole file. And error isolation is already per-file, so one corrupt document in
    ten thousand won't kill the run.

The problems are three:

It's single-threaded. One file at a time on one core. PDF text extraction is CPU-bound and slow (pypdf is the slow part of the whole pipeline), 
    so on a PDF-heavy corpus your wall-clock time is basically sum of every file, sequentially. This is the dominant cost and the biggest lever.
One real memory trap: for a common search term, a single large file can accumulate an unbounded hits list before anything is returned — think
  a spreadsheet where the term appears in 100k cells. v1 collects them all, then prints. That's the one place memory can blow up independent of file size.
Two formats load whole-file: legacy .xls (xlrd has no streaming mode) and .docx (python-docx parses the whole XML tree). Individual files of 
  those types are rarely multi-GB, so it's usually fine — but a pathological one has no guard rail.

Everything else (os.walk, filename sorting) is negligible.

