So, directly answering "how will it handle thousands of files and many gigs":

Memory: it's bounded and won't grow with your corpus. Excel (read_only) and text (line-by-line) are streamed, 
  PDFs are extracted page-by-page, and the --max-per-file cap stops a common term in a giant file from accumulating an unbounded hit list. 
    Proven above: ~51 MB peak against a 318 MB corpus. The only formats that load whole-file are legacy .xls and .docx, and --max-size guards 
      against a pathological one.

Speed: it scales with your cores, and PDFs are the variable. The process pool gives near-linear speedup — 
  I couldn't demonstrate that here because the sandbox has only 1 core, but the mechanism is proven correct and deterministic. 
  On your 8+ core box: text and Excel scan at hundreds-to-thousands of files/sec; PDFs are the slow part, and that's why I wired in pdftotext 
    (and PyMuPDF if you install it) — a fast backend plus multiple cores turns a corpus of thousands of PDFs from "go get coffee" into minutes. 
    Install PyMuPDF (pip install pymupdf) for the fastest path, with the caveat I noted in the header: it's AGPL, so fine internally but check before 
    shipping it in a product.

Two operational notes for your environment. If the tree lives on a network share or a WSL /mnt mount, per-file open latency dominates rather than CPU — bump --workers well above your core count there, since the workers spend most of their time waiting on I/O. And scanned PDFs still need OCR first; they're flagged as "no extractable text" rather than silently skipped, so you can pipe those names into ocrmypdf and re-run.

One architectural point worth raising, since you asked about scale: this is a linear scan, which is the right tool for a one-off or occasional search. 
  If you're going to search the same multi-GB corpus repeatedly, the better pattern is index-once, search-many — extract text once into something like 
  SQLite FTS5 and query that in milliseconds thereafter. If this is going to be a recurring search rather than a one-time sweep, I can restructure it that way 
